Isotope: a centred login screen in the desktop's look.

What: re-lays out the greeter's menu (App::menu in src/greeter.rs) over the
whole output instead of a two-column card 866 px wide:
- top left, the Isotope mark and the "ISOTOPE" wordmark. The mark is
  /usr/share/isotope/greeter-mark.svg (the night brand colours) under a dark
  theme and greeter-mark-day.svg (an ink shell with a deep brand core, the
  day palette's mark) under a light one, so it reads on both soft wallpapers;
- top right, a strip with the keyboard layout chooser, the on-screen keyboard
  toggle, the session chooser, and the network and battery icons;
- top centre, the date and a large clock (about 8.5% of the screen width);
- centre, the user's picture in a circle (or their initial), their name, a
  pill-shaped password field, the caps-lock, authenticating and error lines
  under it, and "Other users ›", which opens the user list (with "Enter name
  manually...");
- bottom, round glass buttons with labels for accessibility (its menu) and,
  when logind is there, suspend, restart and shut down (with their
  confirmation dialogs).
Every action and chooser of the upstream layout is kept; only where they sit
changes. The subsurface now covers the output from its top left (it was
866 px wide, 32 px down), so the dividers and the MAX_WIDTH arithmetic that
assumed the card go. The password field is the blurred region of the surface
(Common::blur_rects), as the card was.

The background (src/greeter/background.rs, std only): for Isotope's two
desktop wallpapers, /usr/share/backgrounds/isotope/isotope-desktop-{night,day}.png
(or the picture their link points at, which is what cosmic-bg records), the
greeter shows the soft copy the image build makes next to them,
isotope-desktop-{night,day}-blur.png (tools/gen-wallpaper.py --blur); any
other picture is shown as it is under a scrim of the theme's background colour
at 45%. Common gets an optional `background_rule` for this (the lock screen
leaves it unset and is unchanged) and reads the soft copy itself, as the
greeter user: it is a world-readable file in the image, not user data. The
text and icons take the theme's colour on its background; the glass is that
background colour, translucent, with a hairline edge, so the greeter follows
the system theme like the desktop does.

The fallback: for an output the user has no wallpaper for, and for every
output before a user is chosen (or with no users), the greeter showed its
built-in picture, res/background.jpg (COSMIC's nebula). A user who has never
logged in has no cosmic-bg state, and the daemon's only fallback,
/var/cache/systemd/home/<user>/login-background (systemd-homed), is listed
under no output name, so it never matched. The greeter now shows the system
default wallpaper instead: the `source` of
/usr/share/cosmic/com.system76.CosmicBackground/v1/all (Isotope's night
picture), the day picture under a light theme as on the desktop, through the
same soft-copy rule (background::fallback_background, read by
App::load_fallback_background at start and when the selected user's theme
mode changes). Common::fallback_scrim says whether that picture needs the
scrim; the built-in picture keeps it when the default cannot be read.

New string: `other-users` in i18n/en (other languages fall back to it).

Why: Isotope's look (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-20-isotope-look-design.md,
"Login screen (cosmic-greeter)", and the login mock in
docs/superpowers/specs/assets/isotope-look/mock.html): the login screen must
look like the desktop it opens, and carries the branded login every variant
has.

Made against cosmic-greeter epoch-1.11.0 (80e8a104b75a), with libcosmic
430f3d48a97d.

